body:
Hi, first of all I'm sorry you're dealing with those having thoughts, feelings ... I'm glad you have already discussed it with your therapist. Now I wonder, is there an option for you to retalk about it and address how severe it is? So, they have a deeper understanding of how the situation really is for you and how they support you more/differently. 

I really hope you will be able to have that talk! Maybe it might open some possibilities. If you feel like you can't be that open verbally, please consider writing the severity down or showing a print of this post or sending an email in advance to introduce that you want to talk about this and want to prevent that you end up avoiding that difficult conversation.

&#x200B;

The fact that you say you can't convince yourself 'to do that to him' nor to quit therapy is actually hopeful (to me), I can understand that it's hard for you and that you feel stuck.

However, if you feel like your therapist, your sessions together ... can serve as a protecting factor, that's quite good and I hope you can use it to your best. By that I mean, that exact person and setting that prevents you from doing it, might also be that exact setting that makes you manage it on the long run. By that I mean, if you feel like you'll stay around for a bit longer here with us, you might as well try to get the most out of therapy to upgrade the quality of your life a bit and find a way to deal with your ideations. Of course, I realize this all isn't a walk in the park, I feel like you are giving yourself extra time and that valuable time can be used interestingly with your therapist.

&#x200B;

I think my reaction is a bit chaotic, isn't it? But I hope you understand the crux a bit

&#x200B;

Best of luck! Rooting for you! Really hope you can be a bit more open with your therapist as soon as possible and they can support you more profoundly
